\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A predicate $P$ is a finite relation on label tuples, and a gap-CSP distinguishes instances whose best satisfiable fraction is above one threshold from those below another. An Abelian embedding maps labels into an Abelian group so that satisfying tuples obey the specified additive relation.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
The exact embedding relation, nontriviality condition, alphabet, and threshold conventions are not supplied; both displayed gaps are retained.

Source-record limitation: The Abelian embedding relation and nontriviality convention needed for the conjectural criterion are not supplied.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can the complexity of $\operatorname{gap-CSP}\text{-}P[1,s]$ differ from that of $\operatorname{gap-CSP}\text{-}P[1-\varepsilon,s]$ only when $P$ admits a nontrivial Abelian embedding? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2024/27}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record open; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine whether the stated complexity change near perfect satisfiability requires a nontrivial additive embedding.

